the two tankers that came under attack  in the Gulf of Oman with a Norwegian  owned but Marshall Islands flagged  frontal tear and the Japanese owned but  Panama flagged  kakuka courageous now u.s. naval forces  in the region said that they received  two separate distress calls one at 12  minutes past 6:00 and morning that's  local time and another at 7:00 a.m. so  shortly afterwards the US Navy guided  missile destroyer the USS Bainbridge  responded to those calls now the US Navy  presence in the region is aimed  primarily warding off any Iranian  closure of the Strait of Hormuz a key  maritime choke point the Straits are one  of the world's most important stretches  of water with one-fifth of the world's  oil passing through that waterway which  at its narrowest it's just 21 miles wide  and that includes crude oil and  liquefied natural gas from energy rich  countries in the Gulf region including  Iran Iraq Kuwait Saudi Arabia Qatar and  the United Arab Emirates conflict in the  Straits has the potential to create  shock waves across the world and that  could drive up the price of crude  today's attack alone caused oil prices  to surge by 4 percent during the 1980s  in the iran-iraq war the so called  tanker war Kuwait's oil tankers were  reflagged under a US flag so that oil  could be transported safely through the  Straits and more recently Iran's  president Hassan rouhani threatened to  close the Straits following the Trump  administration decision to leave the  Iranian nuclear deal
 

